# Why a major chord sounds sweet
subtitle: "Why C, E and G sound sweet together while neighbouring keys grate: their vibrations fall back in step as four to five to six."

## Why a major chord sounds sweet
use: piano_keys
with:
    octaves: 1
    highlight:
      - C4
      - E4
      - G4
      - C#4
    box:
      x: 150
      'y': 130
      w: 500
      h: 300

    card title "Why a major chord sounds sweet"

Why do C, E and G sound so sweet together, when two neighbouring keys grate? The answer is in how fast each note vibrates.
    draw keys labels

Middle C: its string swings back and forth about 262 times a second.
    play "C4:h" tempo 100 instrument piano reveal ["highlight_0"]

Slow that right down and draw a sliver of it: four swings of C.
    path wave_c smooth #b5482e points [[230,480],[241.9,488.5],[253.8,494.7],[265.6,497],[277.5,494.7],[289.4,488.5],[301.3,480],[313.1,471.5],[325,465.3],[336.9,463],[348.8,465.3],[360.6,471.5],[372.5,480],[384.4,488.5],[396.3,494.7],[408.1,497],[420,494.7],[431.9,488.5],[443.8,480],[455.6,471.5],[467.5,465.3],[479.4,463],[491.3,465.3],[503.1,471.5],[515,480],[526.9,488.5],[538.8,494.7],[550.6,497],[562.5,494.7],[574.4,488.5],[586.3,480],[598.1,471.5],[610,465.3],[621.9,463],[633.8,465.3],[645.6,471.5],[657.5,480],[669.4,488.5],[681.3,494.7],[693.1,497],[705,494.7],[716.9,488.5],[728.8,480],[740.6,471.5],[752.5,465.3],[764.4,463],[776.3,465.3],[788.1,471.5],[800,480]]
    text lab_c "C · 4 swings" #b5482e x 95 y 480 font_size 30

E swings faster, about 330 times a second.
    play "E4:h" tempo 100 instrument piano reveal ["highlight_1"]

So in the time C makes four swings, E makes five.
    path wave_e smooth #2f6b8f points [[230,545],[239.5,553.5],[249,559.7],[258.5,562],[268,559.7],[277.5,553.5],[287,545],[296.5,536.5],[306,530.3],[315.5,528],[325,530.3],[334.5,536.5],[344,545],[353.5,553.5],[363,559.7],[372.5,562],[382,559.7],[391.5,553.5],[401,545],[410.5,536.5],[420,530.3],[429.5,528],[439,530.3],[448.5,536.5],[458,545],[467.5,553.5],[477,559.7],[486.5,562],[496,559.7],[505.5,553.5],[515,545],[524.5,536.5],[534,530.3],[543.5,528],[553,530.3],[562.5,536.5],[572,545],[581.5,553.5],[591,559.7],[600.5,562],[610,559.7],[619.5,553.5],[629,545],[638.5,536.5],[648,530.3],[657.5,528],[667,530.3],[676.5,536.5],[686,545],[695.5,553.5],[705,559.7],[714.5,562],[724,559.7],[733.5,553.5],[743,545],[752.5,536.5],[762,530.3],[771.5,528],[781,530.3],[790.5,536.5],[800,545]]
    text lab_e "E · 5" #2f6b8f x 110 y 545 font_size 30

G is faster still, about 392 a second.
    play "G4:h" tempo 100 instrument piano reveal ["highlight_2"]

Six swings in the same stretch.
    path wave_g smooth #8a5fa8 points [[230,610],[237.9,618.5],[245.8,624.7],[253.8,627],[261.7,624.7],[269.6,618.5],[277.5,610],[285.4,601.5],[293.3,595.3],[301.3,593],[309.2,595.3],[317.1,601.5],[325,610],[332.9,618.5],[340.8,624.7],[348.8,627],[356.7,624.7],[364.6,618.5],[372.5,610],[380.4,601.5],[388.3,595.3],[396.3,593],[404.2,595.3],[412.1,601.5],[420,610],[427.9,618.5],[435.8,624.7],[443.8,627],[451.7,624.7],[459.6,618.5],[467.5,610],[475.4,601.5],[483.3,595.3],[491.3,593],[499.2,595.3],[507.1,601.5],[515,610],[522.9,618.5],[530.8,624.7],[538.8,627],[546.7,624.7],[554.6,618.5],[562.5,610],[570.4,601.5],[578.3,595.3],[586.3,593],[594.2,595.3],[602.1,601.5],[610,610],[617.9,618.5],[625.8,624.7],[633.8,627],[641.7,624.7],[649.6,618.5],[657.5,610],[665.4,601.5],[673.3,595.3],[681.3,593],[689.2,595.3],[697.1,601.5],[705,610],[712.9,618.5],[720.8,624.7],[728.8,627],[736.7,624.7],[744.6,618.5],[752.5,610],[760.4,601.5],[768.3,595.3],[776.3,593],[784.2,595.3],[792.1,601.5],[800,610]]
    text lab_g "G · 6" #8a5fa8 x 110 y 610 font_size 30

Four, five, six: at the end of the stretch all three begin a swing together, and the pattern repeats exactly, over and over.
    path sync dashed #8f887c points [[800,455],[800,635]]
    label lab_sync "in step again" right #8f887c attach_to sync

The ear hears waves that keep falling back in step as one smooth, settled sound.
    play "C4+E4+G4:w" tempo 80 instrument piano

    pause 0.4

    erase wave_e lab_e wave_g lab_g sync lab_sync highlight_1 highlight_2

Now C with its neighbour, C sharp. Hear how they rub.
    play "C4+C#4:w" tempo 80 instrument piano reveal ["highlight_3"]

In C's four swings, C sharp makes about four and a quarter. The two slip out of step and take a long time to meet again.
    path wave_cs smooth dashed #d0865f points [[230,545],[241.2,553.5],[252.4,559.7],[263.5,562],[274.7,559.8],[285.9,553.6],[297.1,545.1],[308.2,536.6],[319.4,530.4],[330.6,528],[341.8,530.2],[352.9,536.3],[364.1,544.7],[375.3,553.3],[386.5,559.6],[397.6,562],[408.8,559.9],[420,553.8],[431.2,545.4],[442.4,536.8],[453.5,530.5],[464.7,528],[475.9,530.1],[487.1,536.1],[498.2,544.5],[509.4,553],[520.6,559.4],[531.8,562],[542.9,560],[554.1,554],[565.3,545.6],[576.5,537.1],[587.6,530.6],[598.8,528],[610,529.9],[621.2,535.9],[632.4,544.2],[643.5,552.8],[654.7,559.3],[665.9,562],[677.1,560.1],[688.2,554.2],[699.4,545.9],[710.6,537.3],[721.8,530.8],[732.9,528],[744.1,529.8],[755.3,535.7],[766.5,544],[777.6,552.6],[788.8,559.2],[800,562]]
    text lab_cs "C♯ · 4¼" #d0865f x 110 y 545 font_size 30

    erase wave_cs lab_cs highlight_3

Back to E, and back in step.
    draw wave_e lab_e wave_g lab_g sync lab_sync highlight_1 highlight_2

So the ear likes small whole numbers. C, E and G vibrate almost exactly as four to five to six.
    math ratio tex "C : E : G \\;=\\; 4 : 5 : 6" size 34 colors {"4":"#b5482e","5":"#2f6b8f","6":"#8a5fa8"} x 830 y 290

The Pythagoreans found such ratios on strings 2,500 years ago: a string two thirds as long sounds a fifth higher, like C up to G.
    highlight highlight_0 highlight_2

Try it on the keys: press C, E and G, then C and C sharp, and hear the difference.
    explore

    quiz "Which pair sounds smoothest together?"
        right: C and G: two swings against three, so their waves fall back in step almost at once.
        + C and G — 2 : 3
        * C and C♯ — 15 : 16
        * C and F♯ — 32 : 45

